WEBVTT

NOTE Saying it out loud

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.820
In February 1961, the Joint Chiefs of Staff reviewed a CIA plan to invade Cuba at

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.167
the Bay of Pigs.

3
00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:13.796
Their report listed the plan's weak points, then closed with a judgment.

4
00:00:13.796 --> 00:00:18.000
The plan, it said, "has a fair chance of ultimate success."

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.120
The officer who drafted that line later said the Chiefs put the odds at roughly

6
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.233
three in ten. He meant "fair" as a warning.

7
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:29.628
Other readers heard approval.

8
00:00:29.628 --> 00:00:34.167
The CIA quoted the phrase in a paper that argued for the invasion.

9
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:39.179
President Kennedy came to believe that the Joint Chiefs had endorsed the plan.

10
00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:43.200
After the invasion collapsed, he wondered why no one had warned him.

11
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.599
Three in ten meant seven chances in ten of failure.

12
00:00:47.599 --> 00:00:54.022
Where, between the writer and the reader, did seven in ten disappear, and how do you

13
00:00:54.022 --> 00:00:54.633
stop it?

14
00:00:55.433 --> 00:01:01.981
Uncertainty is not communicated until the listener holds the number the speaker

15
00:01:01.981 --> 00:01:07.700
meant. A message about uncertainty has parts, and each part can fail.

16
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:14.797
The message has six stops: the word, the group a number counts over, the picture,

17
00:01:14.797 --> 00:01:16.589
and the single result.

18
00:01:16.589 --> 00:01:23.430
Then words used on purpose, and the whole statement, with its missing part: how good

19
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.733
the evidence is.

20
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:30.800
First, the word. The trouble began ten years before the Bay of Pigs.

21
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:37.457
In March 1951, a formal intelligence forecast said a Soviet attack on Yugoslavia

22
00:01:37.457 --> 00:01:41.033
"should be considered a serious possibility."

23
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:46.551
Sherman Kent, on the board that approved it, meant about sixty-five in a hundred.

24
00:01:46.551 --> 00:01:49.900
A State Department planner had read it as much lower.

25
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.539
The other board members had meant anything from twenty in a hundred to eighty.

26
00:01:55.539 --> 00:01:59.367
They had agreed on a phrase without agreeing on a number.

27
00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:05.597
Kent's fix was to give each word a number, and such ladders are now official.

28
00:02:05.597 --> 00:02:11.104
For United States intelligence, likely means fifty-five to eighty percent.

29
00:02:11.104 --> 00:02:17.207
For the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, the IPCC, it means sixty-six to

30
00:02:17.207 --> 00:02:18.100
one hundred.

31
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.472
So a sixty percent chance is likely to an intelligence analyst, but only about as

32
00:02:24.472 --> 00:02:27.200
likely as not to a climate scientist.

33
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.159
Readers bend the words too.

34
00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:37.353
In a 2012 study, Americans put very likely, meant as ninety percent or more, at

35
00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:41.633
sixty-two. Very unlikely came out at forty-one.

36
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:48.127
A 2019 study found a fix that works, the number in brackets inside the sentence.

37
00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:54.420
With the word alone, readers' ranges overlapped the official range by about a third.

38
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.267
With the bracket, by about two thirds.

39
00:02:57.767 --> 00:03:01.700
A word carries a number only if the number is beside it.

40
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.267
A number beside the word fixes the word. But a number can fail too.

41
00:03:08.567 --> 00:03:11.950
Take a thirty percent chance of rain tomorrow.

42
00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.730
In 2005, researchers asked people in five cities what that means.

43
00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:22.467
Many said rain thirty percent of the time, or over thirty percent of the area.

44
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:25.656
The weather service means something else.

45
00:03:25.656 --> 00:03:29.467
It rains on about three of every ten days like tomorrow.

46
00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:33.657
In New York, about two thirds chose that meaning.

47
00:03:33.657 --> 00:03:38.067
In four European cities, only one third to one fifth did.

48
00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:43.994
The missing piece is the reference class, the set of cases a number counts over.

49
00:03:43.994 --> 00:03:48.900
A number needs its reference class, or the listener supplies their own.

50
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:55.600
Words and numbers fail in the ear. Pictures fail in the eye.

51
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.232
The clearest case is a hurricane.

52
00:03:58.232 --> 00:04:04.097
The National Hurricane Center draws a cone around a storm's forecast track, to show

53
00:04:04.097 --> 00:04:06.500
where its center will probably go.

54
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.663
Each circle is sized so that two thirds of past forecast errors fall inside.

55
00:04:12.663 --> 00:04:16.833
So about one time in three, the center leaves the cone.

56
00:04:17.233 --> 00:04:23.223
In August 2004, the forecast line for Hurricane Charley ran close to Tampa.

57
00:04:23.223 --> 00:04:28.733
Charley struck Punta Gorda instead, about seventy miles to the south.

58
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:34.585
Charlotte County, around Punta Gorda, had been inside the cone for four days.

59
00:04:34.585 --> 00:04:39.400
But much of the television coverage had pointed viewers at the line.

60
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:45.446
The hurricane center's director, Max Mayfield, later joked about his tombstone:

61
00:04:45.446 --> 00:04:48.233
"Don't focus on the skinny black line."

62
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:54.876
A picture of uncertainty is read as a picture of certainty, unless it says what it

63
00:04:54.876 --> 00:04:55.700
leaves out.

64
00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.933
Suppose the word, the number, the group and the picture are all right.

65
00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:06.262
The listener still judges by one outcome.

66
00:05:06.262 --> 00:05:12.246
In its final 2016 forecast, FiveThirtyEight gave Donald Trump about a twenty-nine

67
00:05:12.246 --> 00:05:14.167
percent chance of winning.

68
00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:19.733
Other models gave him fifteen percent, and some gave him less than one.

69
00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:20.862
Trump won.

70
00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:26.911
But twenty-nine in a hundred is more than one chance in four, and events with those

71
00:05:26.911 --> 00:05:28.733
odds happen all the time.

72
00:05:29.233 --> 00:05:36.100
One outcome cannot judge a probability. Say so before the outcome arrives.

73
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.433
If words fail so often, why not always use numbers?

74
00:05:41.733 --> 00:05:47.321
Some institutions refuse them on purpose. Take accounting.

75
00:05:47.321 --> 00:05:54.740
Under its rule for losses, now called ASC 450, a loss is probable, reasonably

76
00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:56.667
possible, or remote.

77
00:05:57.067 --> 00:06:01.562
The rule defines each word only with other words.

78
00:06:01.562 --> 00:06:06.700
Probable means likely to occur, and remote means slight.

79
00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.960
Each word sets an action.

80
00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.499
A probable loss that can be estimated goes into the accounts.

81
00:06:13.499 --> 00:06:18.633
A reasonably possible one gets a note, and a remote one gets neither.

82
00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.156
Courts have a ladder too.

83
00:06:21.156 --> 00:06:28.201
In Addington against Texas, in 1979, the Supreme Court described three standards of

84
00:06:28.201 --> 00:06:31.767
proof: how sure the judge or jury must be.

85
00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:37.863
In a money dispute, the two sides share the risk of error about equally.

86
00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:41.898
In a criminal case, society takes almost all of it.

87
00:06:41.898 --> 00:06:45.300
Clear and convincing evidence sits between.

88
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.553
The Court put no numbers on these phrases.

89
00:06:48.553 --> 00:06:52.833
It said that how they shape decisions "may well be unknowable."

90
00:06:53.333 --> 00:07:00.133
Some ladders are words on purpose, tied to actions. Know which ladder you are on.

91
00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:07.767
Word, number, group, picture, one result, and the ladder you are on.

92
00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:10.072
One piece is still missing.

93
00:07:10.072 --> 00:07:16.238
In 2019, van der Bles and colleagues set out what a statement of uncertainty should

94
00:07:16.238 --> 00:07:16.832
contain.

95
00:07:16.832 --> 00:07:22.700
It says what you are unsure about, and in what form, such as a range or a word.

96
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.883
Then it says how good the evidence is.

97
00:07:25.883 --> 00:07:31.668
The IPCC calls this confidence, rated by the amount of evidence and how closely

98
00:07:31.668 --> 00:07:32.767
findings agree.

99
00:07:33.167 --> 00:07:36.983
Confidence is a separate scale, not a probability.

100
00:07:36.983 --> 00:07:40.800
It tells the listener how far to trust the number.

101
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.628
Put together, a full statement reads like this.

102
00:07:44.628 --> 00:07:50.244
Rain tomorrow is likely, fifty-five to eighty percent, counted over days like

103
00:07:50.244 --> 00:07:54.767
tomorrow at this spot. Our confidence in that range is medium.

104
00:07:55.267 --> 00:08:01.233
Say the word, the number, the group, and how good the evidence is.

105
00:08:02.233 --> 00:08:08.349
Taken in order, the stops make one argument. A word needs a number beside it.

106
00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:14.067
But a number needs its group, and a picture must say what it leaves out.

107
00:08:14.367 --> 00:08:19.710
Even then, one outcome cannot judge the forecast, so say that first.

108
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:24.738
Some ladders are words on purpose, so know which one you are on.

109
00:08:24.738 --> 00:08:29.767
And every message needs a statement of how good the evidence is.

110
00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:36.764
Where, between the writer and the reader, did seven in ten disappear, and how do you

111
00:08:36.764 --> 00:08:37.374
stop it?

112
00:08:37.374 --> 00:08:43.391
It disappeared beside the word, where no number, no group and no confidence was

113
00:08:43.391 --> 00:08:44.000
written.

114
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.606
Written in full, the line would read like this.

115
00:08:47.606 --> 00:08:52.960
The plan has a fair chance, about three in ten, judged against operations of this

116
00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.067
kind, and our confidence in that number is low.

117
00:08:56.667 --> 00:09:03.222
Fifteen years after the Bay of Pigs, the officer who wrote "a fair chance" was still

118
00:09:03.222 --> 00:09:08.606
troubled. He had not insisted that the numbers be used. Say the word.

119
00:09:08.606 --> 00:09:10.167
Then say the number.
